<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    win11-optimizer.wxs - the installer. Chunk P5-C2 part B, polished in P5-C4.

    WiX v3. candle compiles this to a .wixobj, light links that to a .msi, and
    packaging\Build-Msi.ps1 is the only supported way to run either - it finds
    the toolset, passes the three preprocessor variables this file needs, and
    stops with an explanation if WiX is not installed. There is no fallback
    packager and no hand-rolled .msi: a .msi built by anything other than
    Windows Installer's own tooling is not a .msi, it is a file with the wrong
    extension on it.

    Preprocessor variables (all three supplied by Build-Msi.ps1):
        SourceRoot      the repository root, with no trailing separator
        ProductVersion  three-part version, e.g. 0.1.0
        LicenseRtf      ABSOLUTE path to the generated licence .rtf. Generated
                        into packaging\obj\ from LICENSE.md on every build and
                        never committed, so the licence the installer shows
                        cannot drift from the licence in the repository. It is
                        absolute because a relative path here would resolve
                        against the current directory rather than against this
                        file.

    Linking needs -ext WixUIExtension for the dialog set. Build-Msi.ps1 passes
    it. Dropping it is not a quiet downgrade to no UI - light stops with
    "error LGHT0094 : Unresolved reference to symbol 'Dialog:ErrorDlg'", one per
    dialog, and produces no .msi at all.

    WHAT THIS INSTALLS, and it is a short list on purpose:

      1. The engine module, whole, to
             C:\Program Files\win11-optimizer\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\
         so that
             Import-Module 'C:\Program Files\win11-optimizer\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine'
         works with no path fixing and no PSModulePath change.

      2. LICENSE.md, README.md and USAGE.md to
         C:\Program Files\win11-optimizer\docs\.

      3. One Start Menu shortcut, running Windows PowerShell 5.1 against
         App\Bootstrap.ps1. It does NOT ask for elevation - the menu asks, per
         choice, when a choice needs it.

      4. %ProgramData%\win11-optimizer, created with an explicit ACL and left
         behind on uninstall. This is Q21 and it is the reason this chunk is an
         installer at all: nothing but an installer can set that ACL, and
         without the ACL moving the ledger out of the repo makes it FORGEABLE
         rather than safe. Removal\ActionLog.ps1 checks the ACL at run time and
         refuses to write a ledger into a folder that does not have it.

    WHAT IT LEAVES OUT: tests\, the whole of docs\, logs\, .git\, .gitignore
    and every hidden file. A test asserts that the file list here is
    EXACTLY the engine folder's contents plus those three documents, in both
    directions - a source file added to the module and not added here would
    otherwise install a module that cannot load, and the first person to find
    that out would be a user.

    THE THREE DOCUMENTS ARE THE THREE A PERSON INSTALLING THIS CAN USE, and
    LICENSE.md is there because Apache-2.0 4(a) says every recipient gets a copy
    of the licence and shipping this .msi is distributing the Work. Until P5-C4
    the package shipped docs\CHECKLIST.md, PLAN.md, RESEARCH.md, REVIEW.md and
    STATE.md: the chunk board and the per-chunk research notes, of no use to
    anybody who did not write them. STATE.md is also 91 KB of measured detail
    about the machine this project was developed on - installed software,
    service names, publisher strings - and it was being copied into Program
    Files on every machine that installed the .msi. docs\ is gitignored, which
    is a second reason those five were an odd thing to be shipping.

    The engine folder ships WHOLE - .psm1 and Data\*.json included - and not
    just the .ps1 and .psd1 files the prompt names. The .psm1 IS the module and
    the three .json files are the curated lists every detector loads at startup;
    an install without them is an install that throws on its first scan.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi">

  <?ifndef ProductVersion ?>
  <?define ProductVersion = "0.1.0" ?>
  <?endif ?>

  <!-- The ACL Q21 asks for, as one SDDL string, applied to
       %ProgramData%\win11-optimizer by MsiLockPermissionsEx.

         O:BA                    owner: Administrators
         D:P                     inheritance OFF - and this is the whole point.
                                 %ProgramData%'s own entries grant CREATOR OWNER
                                 full control of what is created below it and let
                                 Users create things there, so a folder that
                                 merely inherits leaves the ledger appendable and
                                 deletable by a standard user.
         (A;OICI;FA;;;BA)        Administrators: Full Control, inherited by
                                 subfolders and files
         (A;OICI;FA;;;SY)        SYSTEM: Full Control, likewise
         (A;OICI;0x1200a9;;;BU)  Users: Read and Execute, likewise. 0x1200a9 is
                                 FILE_GENERIC_READ | FILE_GENERIC_EXECUTE.

       SIDs, not names: BA/SY/BU are the same on every Windows in every language,
       and "Administrators" is not. -->
  <?define LedgerSddl = "O:BAD:P(A;OICI;FA;;;BA)(A;OICI;FA;;;SY)(A;OICI;0x1200a9;;;BU)" ?>

  <!-- MANUFACTURER IS THE PUBLISHER, NOT THE PRODUCT. It used to say
       "win11-optimizer", so Add/Remove Programs read "win11-optimizer by
       win11-optimizer" and the Publisher column was noise. -->
  <Product Id="*"
           Name="win11-optimizer"
           Language="1033"
           Version="$(var.ProductVersion)"
           Manufacturer="Nem702"
           UpgradeCode="{95BED497-2E92-42B8-80D8-6E479D68FCAB}">

    <Package InstallerVersion="500"
             Compressed="yes"
             InstallScope="perMachine"
             Description="win11-optimizer - a console bloatware-removal tool for Windows 11"
             Comments="Installs the engine module, a Start Menu shortcut and the machine-wide action ledger folder." />

    <MajorUpgrade DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of [ProductName] is already installed. Uninstall it first." />
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!-- InstallerVersion 500 is Windows Installer 5.0, which is what
         MsiLockPermissionsEx (the ACL above) needs. Windows 11 ships it. -->
    <Condition Message="win11-optimizer needs Windows 7 or later.">
      <![CDATA[Installed OR (VersionNT >= 601)]]>
    </Condition>

    <!-- ARPNOMODIFY IS SET BY WixUI_Minimal, NOT HERE, and the duplication is
         not allowed: a second <Property Id="ARPNOMODIFY"> in this file is a
         duplicate-symbol error at link time. A test reads the property back out
         of the built .msi so "it is still set" stays a checked claim rather
         than something this comment asserts. -->

    <!-- Add/Remove Programs' support link. It used to be
         https://github.com/win11-optimizer, which is not a repository, not an
         account and not anything else either: ARP renders it as a live link,
         and the first person to click it got a 404. -->
    <Property Id="ARPURLINFOABOUT" Value="https://github.com/Nem702/win11-optimizer" />

    <!-- The icon, declared once and used twice: here for Add/Remove Programs,
         and on the Start Menu shortcut further down.

         THE Id MUST END IN .ico. Windows Installer writes the Icon table's key
         out as a file name when it extracts the icon into the installer cache,
         and a key with no extension produces a cached file that Explorer will
         not read as an icon - ARP then falls back to the generic box this
         exists to replace.

         The file carries 16, 24, 32, 48, 64, 128 and 256 px, so ARP's small
         list icon and the Start Menu's larger one come out of the same file at
         whatever size each asks for. It is a stream in the .msi, not an
         installed file: nothing new lands in Program Files for it. -->
    <Icon Id="win11optimizer.ico" SourceFile="$(var.SourceRoot)\packaging\win11-optimizer.ico" />
    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="win11optimizer.ico" />

    <Directory Id="TARGETDIR" Name="SourceDir">

      <Directory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
        <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="win11-optimizer">
          <Directory Id="SrcFolder" Name="src">
            <Directory Id="EngineFolder" Name="Win11Optimizer.Engine">
              <Directory Id="EngineAppFolder"       Name="App" />
              <Directory Id="EngineDataFolder"      Name="Data" />
              <Directory Id="EngineDetectorsFolder" Name="Detectors" />
              <Directory Id="EngineRemovalFolder"   Name="Removal" />
              <Directory Id="EngineReviewFolder"    Name="Review" />
              <Directory Id="EngineSharedFolder"    Name="Shared" />
              <Directory Id="EngineSupportFolder"   Name="Support" />
            </Directory>
          </Directory>
          <Directory Id="DocsFolder" Name="docs" />
        </Directory>
      </Directory>

      <!-- %ProgramData%. CommonAppDataFolder is Windows Installer's own name
           for it, so this resolves correctly on a machine whose ProgramData has
           been moved off C:. -->
      <Directory Id="CommonAppDataFolder">
        <Directory Id="LedgerFolder" Name="win11-optimizer" />
      </Directory>

      <Directory Id="ProgramMenuFolder" />

    </Directory>

    <Feature Id="ProductFeature" Title="win11-optimizer" Level="1" Absent="disallow">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="CG.Engine.Root" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="CG.Engine.App" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="CG.Engine.Data" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="CG.Engine.Detectors" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="CG.Engine.Removal" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="CG.Engine.Review" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="CG.Engine.Shared" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="CG.Engine.Support" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="CG.Docs" />
      <ComponentRef Id="C_StartMenuShortcut" />
      <ComponentRef Id="C_LedgerFolder" />
    </Feature>

    <!-- WiX's own WixUI_Minimal, unmodified. Its single first-run dialog is
         WelcomeEulaDlg - the welcome page and the licence agreement in one -
         and it renders whatever WixUILicenseRtf points at.

         P5-C4 could not use it: with LICENSE.md empty there was no licence to
         render and WelcomeEulaDlg would have shown WiX's PLACEHOLDER EULA, a
         licence nobody wrote, displayed as though somebody had, and accepted by
         everyone who installed this. So P5-C4 shipped a dialog set of its own,
         WixUI_Minimal's dialogs with the licence page taken out, and wrote down
         that it could all go the day LICENSE.md was filled in. It is filled in.
         That fragment is gone and this is the line it said to put back.

         WHAT COMES WITH THE LICENCE PAGE, and it is worth stating because a
         previous chunk asserted the opposite: WelcomeEulaDlg has a Print
         button, and WiX wires it to WixUIPrintEula - a DLL custom action in
         WixUIExtension's own WixUIWixca binary stream. So the package now
         carries TWO custom actions and a helper DLL where it carried one and
         none. That is not avoidable while showing a licence page: every WiX
         licence dialog has the same Print button. -->
    <UIRef Id="WixUI_Minimal" />

    <!-- The licence page's text. GENERATED FROM LICENSE.md ON EVERY BUILD, into
         packaging\obj\, and never committed: a checked-in .rtf is a second copy
         of the licence that can drift from the first, and the drift would be
         invisible - the installer would show one licence and the file installed
         beside it would say another. Build-Msi.ps1 does the conversion and
         passes the path in as $(var.LicenseRtf). -->
    <WixVariable Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="$(var.LicenseRtf)" />

    <!-- The finish page's two additions, both read by WiX's own ExitDlg.

         WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALTEXT and the checkbox between them answer the
         complaint that the old silent install ended with no sign that anything
         had happened or where the thing had gone.

         The text is a literal. A [PROPERTY] reference here would print as
         itself: ExitDlg formats its control text once, and the value this
         property expands to is not formatted again. -->
    <Property Id="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALTEXT" Value="You will find win11-optimizer in the Start Menu." />
    <Property Id="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOXTEXT" Value="Launch win11-optimizer" />
    <Property Id="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOX" Value="1" />

    <!-- THE ONLY CUSTOM ACTION IN THIS PACKAGE, and the only code the installer
         runs that is not Windows Installer's own.

         IT IS IN NO SEQUENCE TABLE. Its single caller is the ExitDialog Publish
         in the dialog set below, so it runs when a person ticks a box and clicks
         Finish, and at no other moment: nothing during the install executes it,
         and a quiet install (msiexec /qn, which has no UI and therefore no
         finish page) never reaches it at all.

         Type 34 - a directory and a command line - so there is no helper DLL,
         no Binary stream and no WixUtilExtension. The whole action is the text
         below.

         IT RUNS UNELEVATED, which is the same promise the Start Menu shortcut
         makes. An immediate custom action published from a dialog runs in the
         UI process, and for a perMachine package that process is the person's
         own unelevated session - the elevated half of the install is a separate
         process that finished before the finish page appeared.

         asyncNoWait because this starts a console application the person is
         about to use: waiting for it would leave the finish page on screen
         until they quit the menu.

         It runs Bootstrap.ps1 through Windows PowerShell 5.1, character for
         character what the Start Menu shortcut runs, for the reasons written
         out at the shortcut. -->
    <CustomAction Id="CA.LaunchWin11Optimizer"
                  Directory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                  ExeCommand="&quot;[System64Folder]WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe&quot; -NoProfile -File &quot;[#F_App_Bootstrap_ps1]&quot;"
                  Execute="immediate"
                  Impersonate="yes"
                  Return="asyncNoWait" />

    <!-- The launch checkbox's one wire, and the only thing this file adds to
         WixUI_Minimal. A <UI> in the Product merges into the dialog set rather
         than replacing it, which is the whole reason a dialog set of our own is
         no longer needed for this.

         NOT Installed keeps it off the finish page on the way back out:
         "Launch win11-optimizer" at the end of an uninstall would be a lie, and
         the .lnk it points at is gone by then. -->
    <UI>
      <Publish Dialog="ExitDialog" Control="Finish" Event="DoAction" Value="CA.LaunchWin11Optimizer" Order="1">WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOX = 1 AND NOT Installed</Publish>
    </UI>

  </Product>

  <Fragment>

    <ComponentGroup Id="CG.Engine.Root" Directory="EngineFolder">
      <Component Id="C_Win11Optimizer_Engine_psd1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Win11Optimizer_Engine_psd1" Name="Win11Optimizer.Engine.psd1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Win11Optimizer.Engine.psd1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Win11Optimizer_Engine_psm1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Win11Optimizer_Engine_psm1" Name="Win11Optimizer.Engine.psm1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Win11Optimizer.Engine.psm1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
    <ComponentGroup Id="CG.Engine.App" Directory="EngineAppFolder">
      <Component Id="C_App_Bootstrap_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_App_Bootstrap_ps1" Name="Bootstrap.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\App\Bootstrap.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_App_Entry_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_App_Entry_ps1" Name="Entry.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\App\Entry.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_App_Menu_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_App_Menu_ps1" Name="Menu.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\App\Menu.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
    <ComponentGroup Id="CG.Engine.Data" Directory="EngineDataFolder">
      <Component Id="C_Data_junk_locations_json" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Data_junk_locations_json" Name="junk-locations.json" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Data\junk-locations.json" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Data_known_bloatware_json" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Data_known_bloatware_json" Name="known-bloatware.json" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Data\known-bloatware.json" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Data_known_startup_items_json" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Data_known_startup_items_json" Name="known-startup-items.json" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Data\known-startup-items.json" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Data_README_md" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Data_README_md" Name="README.md" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Data\README.md" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Data_unused_app_exclusions_json" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Data_unused_app_exclusions_json" Name="unused-app-exclusions.json" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Data\unused-app-exclusions.json" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
    <ComponentGroup Id="CG.Engine.Detectors" Directory="EngineDetectorsFolder">
      <Component Id="C_Detectors_JunkFiles_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Detectors_JunkFiles_ps1" Name="JunkFiles.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Detectors\JunkFiles.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Detectors_OemBloatware_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Detectors_OemBloatware_ps1" Name="OemBloatware.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Detectors\OemBloatware.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Detectors_README_md" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Detectors_README_md" Name="README.md" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Detectors\README.md" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Detectors_StartupItems_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Detectors_StartupItems_ps1" Name="StartupItems.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Detectors\StartupItems.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Detectors_UnusedApps_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Detectors_UnusedApps_ps1" Name="UnusedApps.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Detectors\UnusedApps.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
    <ComponentGroup Id="CG.Engine.Removal" Directory="EngineRemovalFolder">
      <Component Id="C_Removal_ActionLog_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Removal_ActionLog_ps1" Name="ActionLog.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Removal\ActionLog.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Removal_Dispatcher_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Removal_Dispatcher_ps1" Name="Dispatcher.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Removal\Dispatcher.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Removal_Executor_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Removal_Executor_ps1" Name="Executor.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Removal\Executor.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Removal_RestorePoint_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Removal_RestorePoint_ps1" Name="RestorePoint.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Removal\RestorePoint.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
    <ComponentGroup Id="CG.Engine.Review" Directory="EngineReviewFolder">
      <Component Id="C_Review_Execute_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Review_Execute_ps1" Name="Execute.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Review\Execute.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_Review_Screen_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Review_Screen_ps1" Name="Screen.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Review\Screen.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
    <ComponentGroup Id="CG.Engine.Shared" Directory="EngineSharedFolder">
      <Component Id="C_Shared_Inventory_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Shared_Inventory_ps1" Name="Inventory.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Shared\Inventory.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
    <ComponentGroup Id="CG.Engine.Support" Directory="EngineSupportFolder">
      <Component Id="C_Support_Elevation_ps1" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_Support_Elevation_ps1" Name="Elevation.ps1" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\src\Win11Optimizer.Engine\Support\Elevation.ps1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!-- The three documents a person who installed this can use, and nothing
         else. All three live at the repository root and all three are tracked;
         the five that used to be here lived in docs\, which is gitignored.

         LICENSE.md IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IS NOT THE LICENCE PAGE. Apache-2.0
         4(a) requires giving every recipient of the Work a copy of the licence,
         and shipping this .msi is distributing it. The licence page the
         installer shows is a dialog somebody clicks past; the file is the copy
         they keep. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="CG.Docs" Directory="DocsFolder">
      <Component Id="C_docs_LICENSE_md" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_docs_LICENSE_md" Name="LICENSE.md" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\LICENSE.md" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_docs_README_md" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_docs_README_md" Name="README.md" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\README.md" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="C_docs_USAGE_md" Guid="*">
        <File Id="F_docs_USAGE_md" Name="USAGE.md" Source="$(var.SourceRoot)\USAGE.md" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!-- %ProgramData%\win11-optimizer - the action ledger's folder, and Q21's
         answer.

         PERMANENT, AND THAT IS THE INTERESTING ATTRIBUTE. Uninstalling the tool
         does not delete the record of what it did: a ledger is the only way to
         answer "what happened to this PC, and can it be undone?", and the
         moment somebody most wants that answer is right after they have
         uninstalled something. The folder and its contents are left where they
         are, readable by everyone, for a reinstall or for a person with a text
         editor.

         There is no file in this component: only the folder, with its ACL. The
         ledger itself is created by the tool the first time it records
         something, which is why the component's key path is the directory. -->
    <DirectoryRef Id="LedgerFolder">
      <Component Id="C_LedgerFolder"
                 Guid="{18970A50-709F-475F-A448-5132441D3FA0}"
                 Permanent="yes"
                 KeyPath="yes">
        <CreateFolder>
          <!-- The CORE PermissionEx, not WixUtilExtension's. They are different
               elements with the same name: this one writes the MsiLockPermissionsEx
               table and takes an SDDL string, and util:PermissionEx is a custom
               action that takes a user name and has no Sddl attribute at all.
               candle says so plainly if you reach for the wrong one. -->
          <PermissionEx Sddl="$(var.LedgerSddl)" />
        </CreateFolder>
      </Component>
    </DirectoryRef>

    <!-- The Start Menu shortcut.

         TARGET IS WINDOWS POWERSHELL 5.1, ALWAYS. It is the floor this project
         is written to and the only shell guaranteed to be on the machine;
         detecting PowerShell 7 and preferring it is future scope, and a shortcut
         that silently picks a different shell than the one the test suite last
         ran the menu on is not a small change.

         [System64Folder] is C:\Windows\System32 for this x64 package, which is
         where powershell.exe lives. Spelling it out as [WindowsFolder]System32
         would be wrong for a 32-bit package - the file-system redirector sends
         that to SysWOW64 - so it is named through the property that means what
         it says.

         IT RUNS Bootstrap.ps1, NOT Entry.ps1. A shortcut has no console to
         report an import failure to, and Bootstrap.ps1 is Entry.ps1 with a
         %TEMP% log file wrapped round it for exactly that case.

         NO ELEVATION, deliberately. There is no elevation verb here and none is
         wanted: a tool that demands administrator before it will even list what
         it found teaches people to click through UAC. The menu asks per choice,
         when a choice actually needs it.

         THE ICON IS THE PRODUCT'S, NOT POWERSHELL'S. Without it the .lnk shows
         powershell.exe's own icon - honest about what the shortcut runs, wrong
         about what the person installed. Same Icon table row as ARPPRODUCTICON,
         so there is one .ico in the package and one in the repository.

         WorkingDirectory is deliberately absent - "Start In" blank. Nothing in
         the tool resolves a relative path, and a working directory is one more
         thing that can be wrong.

         THE KEY PATH IS UNDER HKCU, AND THAT IS NOT A SLIP. A non-advertised
         shortcut in the Start Menu is per-user data as far as Windows Installer
         is concerned, and ICE38, ICE43 and ICE57 all fail a component that puts
         one there with a per-machine key path. The .lnk still lands in the ALL
         USERS Start Menu, because this package is perMachine and
         ProgramMenuFolder resolves there; the HKCU value only tells the
         installer whose copy of the shortcut to repair. The alternative was to
         build with -sice:ICE38 -sice:ICE43 -sice:ICE57, and an installer that
         links only with its checks switched off is not one to ship. -->
    <DirectoryRef Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Component Id="C_StartMenuShortcut" Guid="{66B49324-B32B-46B3-9685-40034F73892B}">
        <Shortcut Id="StartMenuShortcut"
                  Name="win11-optimizer"
                  Description="Find and remove Windows 11 bloatware, with a record of everything it does."
                  Target="[System64Folder]WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe"
                  Arguments="-NoProfile -File &quot;[#F_App_Bootstrap_ps1]&quot;"
                  Icon="win11optimizer.ico"
                  IconIndex="0"
                  Show="normal" />
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\win11-optimizer"
                       Name="StartMenuShortcut"
                       Type="integer"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </DirectoryRef>
  </Fragment>

</Wix>
